re.\n\n— Summary-judgment strategy & filing position —\n• Lead argument: no genuine dispute on causation. Ortiz's timeline shows the March 3 setpoint change, eleven firmware warnings, and an adequate-warning alternative.\n• Secondary argument: plaintiff's design-defect theory lacks a feasible alternative design. Harlow's expert report identifies none.\n• Do not rely on Voss's fatigue data in the motion given the designation decision; cite exemplar testing only through Kestrel's internal QA records.\n• Daniel drafts the causation section; Rachel finalizes the statement of undisputed facts; Nadia reviews tomorrow for filing prep.\n\n— Witness strategy: Harlow engineer deposition —\n• Primary goals: the shared maintenance login, and who raised the setpoint on March 3.\n• Sequence: foundation and qualifications first, then the inspection timeline, then the logs. Hold the maintenance-record exhibits for the second half.\n• Daniel takes the deposition; Rachel second-chairs. Dates still pending confirmation from opposing counsel.\n\n— Draft declarations —\n• QA lead declaration (v3): tighten paragraph 7 on lot-testing protocol, remove the speculative sentence on field conditions in paragraph 11, and conform the exhibit references to the statement of undisputed facts. Thomas to confirm the QA lead's title and tenure dates.\n• Field technician declaration (v1): Rachel to do a first-pass edit; circulate to the case team via the DMS.\n\n— Settlement authority —\n• Client CEO Gerald Whitfield has authorized a settlement range ahead of the May 12 mediation; Thomas holds the specifics.\n\n— Action items —\n• Daniel Okafor — draft SJ causation section; confirm deposition dates with opposing counsel.\n• Rachel Moreno — finish cite check; prepare Ortiz designation disclosure; first-pass edit on field technician declaration.\n• Thomas Grant — confirm QA lead title and tenure dates; confirm the QA sampling language.\n• Nadia Bennett — review SJ draft tomorrow for filing prep.\n\n— End